{"path":"docs/concepts/workloads/pods/downward-api/index.html","title":"Downward API","description":"It is sometimes useful for a container to have information about itself, without being overly coupled to Kubernetes. The downward API allows containers to consume information about themselves or the…","body":{"type":"document","children":[{"type":"heading","level":1,"id":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Downward API"}]},{"type":"text","value":"There are two ways to expose Pod and container fields to a running container: environment variables, and as files that are populated by a special volume type. Together, these two ways of exposing Pod and container fields are called the downward API."},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is sometimes useful for a container to have information about itself, without being overly coupled to Kubernetes. The "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"downward API"}]},{"type":"text","value":" allows containers to consume information about themselves or the cluster without using the Kubernetes client or API server."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An example is an existing application that assumes a particular well-known environment variable holds a unique identifier. One possibility is to wrap the application, but that is tedious and error-prone, and it violates the goal of low coupling. A better option would be to use the Pod's name as an identifier, and inject the Pod's name into the well-known environment variable."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Kubernetes, there are two ways to expose Pod and container fields to a running container:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":null,"items":[{"children":[{"type":"text","value":"as "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/inject-data-application/environment-variable-expose-pod-information/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"environment variables"}]}]},{"children":[{"type":"text","value":"as "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/inject-data-application/downward-api-volume-expose-pod-information/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"files in a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"downwardAPI"},{"type":"text","value":" volume"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Together, these two ways of exposing Pod and container fields are called the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"downward API"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"available-fields","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Available fields"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/workloads/pods/downward-api/index.html#available-fields","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only some Kubernetes API fields are available through the downward API. This section lists which fields you can make available."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can pass information from available Pod-level fields using "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"fieldRef"},{"type":"text","value":". At the API level, the "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"spec"},{"type":"text","value":" for a Pod always defines at least one "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubernetes-api/workload-resources/pod-v1/#Container","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Container"}]},{"type":"text","value":". You can pass information from available Container-level fields using "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"resourceFieldRef"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"downwardapi-fieldRef","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Information available via "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"fieldRef"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/workloads/pods/downward-api/index.html#downwardapi-fieldRef","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For some Pod-level fields, you can provide them to a container either as an environment variable or using a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"downwardAPI"},{"type":"text","value":" volume. The fields available via either mechanism are:"}]},{"type":"definition_list","items":[{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"metadata.name"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the pod's name"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"metadata.namespace"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the pod's "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/namespaces","title":"An abstraction used by Kubernetes to support isolation of groups of resources within a single cluster.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"namespace"}]}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"metadata.uid"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the pod's unique ID"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"metadata.annotations['<KEY>']"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the value of the pod's "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/annotations","title":"A key-value pair that is used to attach arbitrary non-identifying metadata to objects.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"annotation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" named "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"<KEY>"},{"type":"text","value":" (for example, "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"metadata.annotations['myannotation']"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"metadata.labels['<KEY>']"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the text value of the pod's "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/labels","title":"Tags objects with identifying attributes that are meaningful and relevant to users.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"label"}]},{"type":"text","value":" named "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"<KEY>"},{"type":"text","value":" (for example, "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"metadata.labels['mylabel']"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following information is available through environment variables "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"but not as a downwardAPI volume fieldRef"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"definition_list","items":[{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"spec.serviceAccountName"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the name of the pod's "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-service-account/","title":"Provides an identity for processes that run in a Pod.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"service account"}]}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"spec.nodeName"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the name of the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/nodes/","title":"A node is a worker machine in Kubernetes.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"node"}]},{"type":"text","value":" where the Pod is executing"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"status.hostIP"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the primary IP address of the node to which the Pod is assigned"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"status.hostIPs"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the IP addresses is a dual-stack version of "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"status.hostIP"},{"type":"text","value":", the first is always the same as "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"status.hostIP"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"status.podIP"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the pod's primary IP address (usually, its IPv4 address)"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"status.podIPs"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"the IP addresses is a dual-stack version of "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"status.podIP"},{"type":"text","value":", the first is always the same as "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"status.podIP"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following information is available through a "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"downwardAPI"},{"type":"text","value":" volume "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"fieldRef"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"but not as environment variables"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"definition_list","items":[{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"metadata.labels"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"all of the pod's labels, formatted as "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"label-key=\"escaped-label-value\""},{"type":"text","value":" with one label per line"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"metadata.annotations"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"all of the pod's annotations, formatted as "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"annotation-key=\"escaped-annotation-value\""},{"type":"text","value":" with one annotation per line"}]}]},{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"downwardapi-resourceFieldRef","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Information available via "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"resourceFieldRef"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/workloads/pods/downward-api/index.html#downwardapi-resourceFieldRef","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These container-level fields allow you to provide information about "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/#requests-and-limits","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"requests and limits"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for resources such as CPU and memory."}]},{"type":"heading","level":4,"id":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":"FEATURE STATE: "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"Kubernetes v1.35 [stable]"},{"type":"text","value":"(enabled by default)"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Container CPU and memory resources can be resized while the container is running. If this happens, a downward API volume will be updated, but environment variables will not be updated unless the container restarts. See "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/resize-container-resources/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resize CPU and Memory Resources assigned to Containers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for more details."}]},{"type":"definition_list","items":[{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"resource: limits.cpu"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"A container's CPU limit"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"resource: requests.cpu"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"A container's CPU request"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"resource: limits.memory"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"A container's memory limit"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"resource: requests.memory"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"A container's memory request"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"resource: limits.hugepages-*"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"A container's hugepages limit"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"resource: requests.hugepages-*"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"A container's hugepages request"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"resource: limits.ephemeral-storage"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"A container's ephemeral-storage limit"}]},{"id":null,"term":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"resource: requests.ephemeral-storage"}],"definition":[{"type":"text","value":"A container's ephemeral-storage request"}]}]},{"type":"heading","level":4,"id":"fallback-information-for-resource-limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fallback information for resource limits"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/workloads/pods/downward-api/index.html#fallback-information-for-resource-limits","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If CPU and memory limits are not specified for a container, and you use the downward API to try to expose that information, then the kubelet defaults to exposing the maximum allocatable value for CPU and memory based on the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/#node-allocatable","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"node allocatable"}]},{"type":"text","value":" calculation."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"what-s-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What's next"},{"type":"link","href":"docs/concepts/workloads/pods/downward-api/index.html#what-s-next","title":null,"children":[]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can read about "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/#downwardapi","title":null,"children":[{"type":"inline_code","code":"downwardAPI"},{"type":"text","value":" volumes"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can try using the downward API to expose container- or Pod-level information:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":null,"items":[{"children":[{"type":"text","value":"as "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/inject-data-application/environment-variable-expose-pod-information/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"environment variables"}]}]},{"children":[{"type":"text","value":"as "},{"type":"link","href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/inject-data-application/downward-api-volume-expose-pod-information/","title":null,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"files in "},{"type":"inline_code","code":"downwardAPI"},{"type":"text","value":" volume"}]}]}]}]}}
